  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small, qualitative sample of 20 self-selected residents in a single metropolitan area, which may not represent the broader population's gardening constraints and motivations.",
      "what": "This qualitative study analyzed 20 semi-structured interviews with residents across 13 London boroughs to identify the behavioral drivers of domestic garden rewilding. Using the COM-B framework, the research maps how psychological capability, physical and social opportunities, and reflective and automatic motivations shape residents' gardening practices.",
      "why": "It identifies specific, actionable leverage points for local governments and schools to foster urban rewilding, shifting the focus from general environmental awareness to addressing concrete barriers like information navigation, local community dynamics, and early-years ecological education."
    }
